In practice, replacing the actual values in a nested JSON payload’s config yields a sanitized payload: the same structure and field names, but all sensitive values replaced with placeholders. The idea is to keep the shape of the data so systems can still parse it, while masking secrets, keys, IPs, and other personal or confidential data.

What typically gets redacted (often recursively in nested objects):
- Credentials and keys (api_key, secret, password, token, etc.)
- IP addresses, hostnames, and any identifying device/location data
- Personal data or customer identifiers if present in the config
- Any other values marked as sensitive

Example

Original (conceptual):
{
  "diagnostic": {
    "metadata": {
      "timestamp": "2026-09-24T12:34:56Z",
      "device_id": "DEV-98765",
      "location": "HQ-01"
    },
    "config": {
      "endpoint": "https://api.service.local",
      "credentials": {
        "api_key": "sk_live_abc123",
        "secret": "shhh-its-a-secret"
      },
      "limits": {
        "max_connections": 50,
        "timeout_ms": 15000
      }
    }
  }
}

Redacted (sanitized):
{
  "diagnostic": {
    "metadata": {
      "timestamp": "REDACTED",
      "device_id": "REDACTED",
      "location": "REDACTED"
    },
    "config": {
      "endpoint": "<REDACTED>",
      "credentials": {
        "api_key": "<REDACTED>",
        "secret": "<REDACTED>"
      },
      "limits": {
        "max_connections": 50,
        "timeout_ms": 15000
      }
    }
  }
}

Notes:
- You can use consistent tokens like "<REDACTED>" or "<MASKED>".
- If desired, you can redact only sensitive parts and leave non-sensitive values as-is (e.g., non-confidential numeric limits or timestamps).

If you share a sample payload (with sensitive parts redacted on your end), I can show you exactly how the redaction would look for the rest of the fields.

